<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mayflower</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1620">1620</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mayflower was an English merchant ship that carried about 102 passengers, among them the Separatists later called the Pilgrims, from Plymouth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> in 1620. Bound for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a> region, it reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-cod">Cape Cod</a> in November and the settlers founded Plymouth Colony. The ship returned to England in 1621 and is a central symbol of American founding history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mayflower.jpg" alt="Mayflower" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copyrighted_and_Published_by_A_S_Burbank,_The_Mayflower_at_Sea_(NBY_21340).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Mayflower was an English square-rigged merchant ship of about 180 tons, roughly 110 feet long and 25 feet in the beam, active from before 1609 until 1622. Her passengers included Separatists from a congregation of about 400 English Protestants who had gone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> in 1608 to worship outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, which they judged beyond reform. Others on board were hired hands, servants and farmers recruited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> merchants. The congregation's leader John Robinson stayed in Leiden with those who could not go.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The plan was for the small Speedwell to carry the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> group to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and for the Mayflower to take most passengers and supplies across the Atlantic. The Mayflower left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in mid-July 1620 with about 65 passengers and waited at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a>. Speedwell sprang leaks three times, forcing the ships back to Dartmouth and then to Plymouth in England, and was abandoned, with 20 of her passengers crowded onto the Mayflower.</p><p>The ship sailed on 26 September 1620 with 102 passengers and a crew of about 30. The living area was about 20 by 80 feet with a ceiling of about five feet. Western gales slowed the crossing to more than two months. The ship sighted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-cod">Cape Cod</a> on 19 November 1620, in the old-style calendar, and anchored in what is now Provincetown Harbor on 21 November.</p><p>Before landing, the male Pilgrims and the other passengers, whom the Pilgrims called strangers, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayflower-compact">Mayflower Compact</a>, which set up a rudimentary government. The first winter at Plymouth was severe, with frozen ground, no shelter prepared and illness, and about half the crew died. William Bradford wrote that some who died took the origin of their death on an early exploring trip. The ship sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 15 April 1621, in the old-style calendar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>No record of the ship survives after 1624, when she was appraised at Rotherhithe at 128 pounds, eight shillings and fourpence. Charles Edward Banks held that she was broken up, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a> bought from a Rotherhithe shipbreaker's yard in 1624 were used by Thomas Russell to build the Mayflower Barn at Jordans in Buckinghamshire, though this is not certain.</p><p>Bradford's journal and letters are the main source for the voyage and for Plymouth Colony. The harvest feast of 1621, attended by 90 Wampanoag people, is regarded as the origin of the American Thanksgiving. Richard Bevan calls the crossing the most culturally important of the voyages to the American colonies between 1620 and 1640.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mayflower” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An exploring party from the <b>Mayflower</b> landed at Plymouth harbour, where the Pilgrims decided to settle (11 December Old Style).</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-21">21 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Anchored off Cape Cod, 41 male passengers of the <b>Mayflower</b> signed a compact binding themselves into a 'civil body politic'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-21">21 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Mayflower</b> left Plymouth with 102 passengers, Separatist 'Pilgrims' and others (6 September Old Style).</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-16">16 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>English Separatists who had left the Church of England, many of them after years of exile in Leiden, sailed on the <b>Mayflower</b> in 1620 and landed at Plymouth on Cape Cod Bay.</blockquote><a href="/state/plymouth-colony">Plymouth Colony</a></li></ul></section>
